From 806cf9852450dd0fb7aa2cce469f905873d10b55 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 05:14:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] measure: falsify the pointing hypothesis for 262006

262006 is solvable offline with zero truth violations and 0.693 rad of
field-of-view margin, yet ninety-odd upper-level position cells all die on
field of view, which suggested its failure was a pointing failure a position
channel cannot address. Twelve fixed-setpoint episodes across the three
attitude reference modes test that directly.

The hypothesis does not survive in its simple form. All three modes fail on
262006, and the aimed mode fails sooner rather than later -- 34.9 s becomes
10.7 s at h20 and 29.7 s becomes 8.5 s at h35. So the attitude reference is
struck from the candidate factor queue and 262006 becomes a case-level
limitation rather than something with a proposed fix.

Two things the probe does establish. On 262005 the aimed mode does remove
field of view as the failure mode -- 96.0 s to the 299.9 s time limit at h20,
30.1 s to 211.1 s at h35 -- but it trades it for a timeout and for being
pushed out to 29.96 m against the 30 m limit, so pointing authority is
borrowed from translation, which is expensive in a regime where co-rotation
already needs the full three-axis authority. And frozen and swept are
numerically identical in all four matched pairs, because a fixed setpoint's
reference sightline does not sweep; the repository's "best of three" should
read as best of two distinct behaviours.

The run record keeps every scalar the document cites rather than the 7.5 MB
of per-step traces, and names the command that regenerates them.

